INT. OBSERVATORY DOME - NIGHT

A brass telescope as long as a bus points at a slit of stars. The floor is parquet, the walls lined with star charts and one tipped-over teacup. On the floor beside the eyepiece platform lies a coat, empty, its sleeves flung wide.

Five people stand in a loose ring around it, and nobody wants to be the first to look down.

HENRIETTA "HETTY" BLACKWOOD (60s, observatory director, tweed, a magnifying glass on a chain) stares at the coat. BASTIAN ODUYA (30s, graduate student, ink on his fingers, a wool hat pulled low). SOLEDAD "SOLI" VARGAS (40s, night technician, thermos in one hand and the dome keys in the other). WILFRED PRATT (70s, retired astronomer, a cane, a rumpled evening jacket). And MEI-LING TAN (20s, journalist, notebook, recorder held out like a candle).

                    HETTY
          Who last saw Doctor Marrow?

                    BASTIAN
          Soli did. She locked up at nine.

                    SOLI
          I locked the north door. I did not lock the dome.

                    PRATT
          Soledad, my dear, a dome has no door. It is a lid.

                    SOLI
          Then I locked the lid, Mr. Pratt.

                    MEI-LING
          Let's slow down. Soli, you said Doctor Marrow asked you for the keys?

                    SOLI
          He asked. I said no.

                    BASTIAN
          You told me you gave them to him.

                    SOLI
          I told you he asked, Bash. You heard what you wanted.

                    HETTY
          Bastian. Where were you at nine?

                    BASTIAN
          Plate room. Cataloguing.

                    PRATT
          For five hours? That's not cataloguing, it's meditation.

                    BASTIAN
          It was the Hartwell plates, Mr. Pratt. You know how many there are.

                    PRATT
          I know how many there were. I counted them in 1989.

                    MEI-LING
          Mr. Pratt, you told me earlier you hadn't been to the observatory in years.

                    PRATT
          Did I?

                    MEI-LING
          Your exact words were, "I haven't set foot on this hill since the Marrow appointment."

                    PRATT
          Then I was speaking figuratively.

                    HETTY
          Wilfred. You have a key.

                    PRATT
          Everyone has a key, Hetty. That's the tragedy of the institution.

                    SOLI
          I don't think everyone has a key.

                    HETTY
          Soli, who does?

                    SOLI
          Me. You. Doctor Marrow.

                    BASTIAN
          And Pratt, apparently.

                    PRATT
          Apparently.

                    MEI-LING
          So four keys. And a coat on the floor.

                    HETTY
          Don't touch the coat, Miss Tan.

                    MEI-LING
          I wasn't going to.

                    SOLI
          You were. You've been inching toward it for five minutes.

                    MEI-LING
          I'm a reporter. Inching is the job.

                    PRATT
          Inching is how the best of us died, my girl.

                    HETTY
          Wilfred, enough.

                    PRATT
          I'm only saying. The coat is on the floor, the lid is open, and Doctor Marrow is not in either of them. Somebody in this room is going to have to explain that. And I would very much like for it not to be me.

                    BASTIAN
          Then explain why your cane is wet.

A silence. The telescope creaks.

                    PRATT
          It rained.

                    SOLI
          It hasn't rained since Tuesday.

                    HETTY
          Wilfred.

                    PRATT
          Hetty.

                    HETTY
          Where is he?
